    "result_text": "The full test suite passes \u2014 **284 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field on owner creation. When an owner is created, they now get a `locality` of `'local'` if their city is the single most common city among existing owners at that moment, otherwise `'remote'`.\n\nFollowing the existing computed-field pattern in this OfficeFloor-based codebase (`namesakeCount`, `sharesHousehold`, `customerCode`, etc.):\n\n1. **`ComputeLocality.java`** (new function) \u2014 tallies existing owners by city (excluding the not-yet-saved owner), finds the max count, and sets `locality` to `local` only when there's a *unique* winning city that matches this owner's city (case- and whitespace-insensitive, consistent with the other city functions); ties or no existing owners yield `remote`.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `computeLocality` into the pipeline after `computeSharesHousehold` and before `save`, under the same transaction governance.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the persisted `locality` field with getter/setter (`@Column(name = \"locality\")`), so it survives the round-trip read via `GET /api/owners/{id}` that the test uses.\n\n4. **Schema files** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `locality VARCHAR(10)` column.\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `locality` read-only property to the `Owner` schema, so MapStruct name-maps it into `OwnerDto` and it appears in responses.\n\nThe `Cp18Tests` acceptance test (and all others, including the related `Cp17Tests`) pass.",
